<p>Virtue and the Doctrine of the Mean</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/a-bird-on-a-stick.jpg" alt="A circular archery target with a single arrow piercing its center and an array of concentric circles representing scoring zones." width="860" height="320" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>If eudaimonia is activity of the soul in accordance with reason, performed well, the question becomes: what does that look like in practice? Aristotle's answer is virtue.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    Two kinds of virtue
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Aristotle opens Book II by dividing virtue into two kinds: intellectual and moral.</p>
<p><strong>Intellectual virtues</strong> are excellences of thought: things like wisdom, understanding, and practical judgment. These are acquired mostly through teaching. They take time, because thinking well about complex matters takes experience, but the basic method of acquiring them is instruction.</p>
<p><strong>Moral virtues</strong> are excellences of character: things like courage, generosity, honesty, justice, temperance. These are not acquired through teaching. You cannot become courageous by listening to a lecture on courage. Moral virtues are acquired through <em>habit</em>.</p>
<p>This is a distinction worth lingering on. Aristotle is saying that there is a kind of knowledge you can get from a book (intellectual virtue) and a kind of knowledge you cannot (moral virtue). Moral knowledge lives in your habits, your reactions, your dispositions. You can read about generosity for a year, but that will not make you generous. What makes you generous is acting generously, over and over, until acting that way becomes second nature.</p>
<p>The Greek word Aristotle uses for this settled, habituated state of character is <em>hexis</em>. A hexis is not a feeling you happen to have; it is a stable disposition to feel, think, and act in certain ways. Virtues are <em>hexeis</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    How we get virtues: habituation
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Aristotle draws an analogy that is worth sitting with for a moment (and then doing something about). Nobody is born able to play the lyre. You become a good lyre player by playing the lyre, over and over, under the guidance of someone who already plays well. You learn by doing, and in particular, by doing the right thing repeatedly until doing it becomes automatic. The same, Aristotle says, goes for virtue.</p>
<p>"Men become builders by building," he writes, "and lyre players by playing the lyre; so too we become just by doing just acts, temperate by doing temperate acts, brave by doing brave acts."</p>
<p>This claim has a strange feature that is easy to miss. How do you do a just act before you are just? How do you do a brave act before you are brave? Aristotle acknowledges the puzzle. His answer is that there is a difference between <em>doing the act that a virtuous person would do</em> and <em>doing it the way a virtuous person would do it</em>. At first, you are doing the former. You perform the just act because someone told you to, or because you are imitating someone, or because you are figuring out what justice requires in this case. Over time, through repetition, something shifts. You begin to do the just act <em>because it is just</em>, not because you were told to, and you begin to take pleasure in doing it. That is when you have become just.</p>
<p>This is why Aristotle thinks moral education matters so much. The habits you form early are the habits you will have later. Children who are raised in households where honesty is practiced will find honesty natural as adults. Children raised around cruelty or indifference will not. There is nothing fatalistic here (adults can and do change), but it is harder to build a good character late than early, and Aristotle is clear-eyed about that.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>TERM TO KNOW</p>
<p><em>Hexis</em> (Greek): a settled, stable disposition. For Aristotle, a virtue is not an action or a feeling, but a <em>hexis</em>: a reliable readiness to act, feel, and respond in a certain way. Your hexis is who you are, in the relevant sense.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    The doctrine of the mean
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Once you know what a virtue <em>is</em> (a stable disposition acquired by habituation), you can ask what distinguishes a virtuous disposition from a vicious one. Aristotle's answer is the <em>doctrine of the mean</em>.</p>
<p>The basic idea is this. Every virtue sits between two vices: a vice of excess and a vice of deficiency. The virtue is the mean between them. Aristotle is not talking about a mathematical average. He is talking about getting things <em>right</em>: the right response, at the right time, toward the right person, for the right reason.</p>
<p>Courage is the clearest example. Courage is the mean between cowardice and rashness. A coward feels too much fear; he runs from dangers he should face. A rash person feels too little fear; he charges into dangers he should avoid. The courageous person feels the fear that is appropriate to the situation and acts well in light of it. Courage is not the absence of fear; it is fear properly calibrated.</p>
<p>Consider more examples:</p>
<div>
