</div>
<p>Two clarifications about the doctrine of the mean.</p>
<p>First, the mean is <em>relative to the person and the situation</em>. The right amount of food for a small person is not the right amount for a large person. The right level of assertiveness at your mother's funeral is not the right level at a business negotiation. Aristotle uses the example of Milo, a famous athlete, who needed far more food than an ordinary person. The mean is not a fixed quantity; it is getting it right, <em>for this person, in this situation</em>.</p>
<p>Second, not every action admits of a mean. Aristotle is explicit that some actions are just wrong, period. Murder, theft, adultery: there is no virtuous amount of these. You cannot commit the right amount of murder at the right time toward the right person. The doctrine of the mean applies to <em>capacities and feelings that admit of degree</em>: fear, anger, confidence, pleasure, appetite. Not everything morally significant is like that.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    How do you know where the mean is?
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Here is where students often get frustrated with Aristotle. The doctrine of the mean sounds helpful in the abstract, but how do you actually find the mean in a particular case? How do you know when fear is properly calibrated, when generosity becomes wastefulness, when an honest remark becomes a cruel one?</p>
<p>Aristotle's answer will not satisfy everyone, but it is honest. There is no formula. There is no rule you can consult. The only thing that reliably identifies the mean is the judgment of a <em>phronimos</em>: a person of practical wisdom.</p>
<p>Practical wisdom (<em>phronesis</em>) is the intellectual virtue that allows a person to perceive what the situation calls for and act accordingly. A phronimos has enough experience of life, and has reflected enough on that experience, to see a moral situation clearly and respond well. When we want to know what courage requires <em>here, now, in this case</em>, we look at what a courageous person of good judgment would do.</p>
<p>This is sometimes mocked as circular: the virtuous action is what the virtuous person does, and the virtuous person is the one who does virtuous actions. But Aristotle is making a subtler point. He is saying that ethical knowledge is not the kind of knowledge that can be reduced to rules. You cannot write a book that will tell you what the right thing to do is in every situation. What you can do is cultivate the perceptual skill to <em>see</em> what situations call for, and that skill is developed through experience, reflection, and good company.</p>
<p>If this seems like a weakness of Aristotle's ethics, notice that every ethical theory runs into a version of it eventually. Kant will give us a rule (the categorical imperative), but applying the rule to particular cases requires judgment Kant does not fully explain. Mill will tell us to maximize happiness, but calculating consequences in real cases requires judgment Mill does not fully explain. Aristotle is the thinker who is most honest about this: ethical life is the life of a skilled perceiver, not a rule-follower.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
